# Claude Code Configuration — Python Project

<!-- BEGIN BLUEPRINT -->

## Project identity

- **Language**: Python 3.11+
- **Package manager**: `uv` (preferred) / `pip`
- **Lint**: Flake8 + Black + isort (enforced via pre-commit)
- **Test**: pytest + pytest-cov
- **Type-check**: mypy (strict where feasible)

## Behavioral rules (always enforced)

- Do what was asked — nothing more, nothing less.
- NEVER create files unless necessary. Prefer editing existing ones.
- NEVER create docs/`*.md`/README files unless explicitly requested.
- ALWAYS read a file before editing it.
- NEVER commit secrets, credentials, or `.env` files.

## TDD workflow (mandatory for bugs + new features)

1. Write a **failing test** first. Run it. See it fail.
2. Write the minimum code to make it pass.
3. Refactor. Keep tests green.

If TDD is impractical (e.g. CLI smoke test, config migration), state why before implementing.

## Code conventions

- **Style**: PEP 8 via Flake8 — `max-line-length = 100`, `ignore = E203, W503` (Black compat).
- **Formatting**: Black (88 cols default overridden to 100), isort with `profile = "black"`.
- **Types**: type hints on all public functions. `from __future__ import annotations` at top of each module.
- **Docstrings**: Google style, only when the WHY is non-obvious.
- **File naming**: `snake_case.py`. Classes `PascalCase`. Functions/vars `snake_case`. Constants `UPPER_SNAKE`.
- **Imports**: absolute imports from package root. No wildcard imports.

## File organization

- `/src/<package>/` — source
- `/tests/` — mirrors src layout (`tests/test_foo.py` tests `src/foo.py`)
- `/scripts/` — one-off utilities
- `/docs/` — markdown docs
- Never put working files in the repo root

## Build & test

```bash
# Install
uv sync  # or: pip install -e ".[dev]"

# Lint + format
pre-commit run --all-files
flake8 src tests
black src tests
isort src tests

# Type-check
mypy src

# Test
pytest                          # all
pytest tests/test_foo.py -xvs   # single file
pytest --cov=src --cov-report=term-missing
```

Run ALL of: `pre-commit run --all-files && pytest` before reporting a task complete.

## Pre-commit hooks

Install once per clone: `pre-commit install`. Config lives at `.pre-commit-config.yaml` (copy from `pre-commit/.pre-commit-config.python.yaml` in the blueprint).

## Git

- Branch from `main` / `develop`. Conventional commits: `feat(scope): ...`, `fix(scope): ...`, `test(scope): ...`, `chore(scope): ...`.
- One feature per PR. Rebase, don't merge-commit.
- **Use git worktrees** for parallel tracks: `git worktree add ../proj-feat-x feat/x`. Never stash-and-swap on one checkout.

## Security

- Input validation via `pydantic` at system boundaries.
- Parameterized queries only — never f-string SQL.
- Secrets via env vars + `pydantic-settings`. Never in source.
- Sanitize file paths for any upload / download endpoint.

## Waiting for operator input (multi-agent flow)

If you need a decision from the operator and other agents are running in parallel elsewhere, **don't just ask and return** — block and poll the dashboard inbox so you can resume yourself when they reply:

```
/wait-for-reply "Which DB connection should this pipeline use?"
```

This emits a notification (you show up in the dashboard **Meeting Room**), then polls `~/.claude-agency/inbox/<session>.txt` for up to 120s. When the operator clicks your card and sends a reply, you pick it up automatically and continue in the same turn.

For non-blocking checks at the start of a turn, `/check-inbox` pulls any pending reply without waiting.

Don't use `/wait-for-reply` for trivial questions — only when you're genuinely blocked.

## When working on this project

- Use **serena** MCP for code navigation before grepping large modules.
- Use **context7** MCP for up-to-date docs of third-party libraries.
- Use **playwright** MCP only if this project has a web UI.
- Invoke the `tester` subagent for writing test suites, the `reviewer` subagent for pre-PR review.

## Backend guardrails (non-negotiable)
- Never run migrations against any DB. Generate migration files only; humans apply them.
- Never run queries against prod/staging. Read-only local replicas only, referenced by `DATABASE_URL`.
- Never modify CI workflows without a linked ticket. CI changes are blast-radius.
- PRs touching `migrations/`, `auth/`, `billing/`, or `*_consumer.py` require a human architect review before merge, even if tests pass.

<!-- END BLUEPRINT -->
